From: noname <noname@example.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 10:45:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 18/20] stop concealing the hole a queue drop opens

The play queue holds 12 packets of 5 ms, and push_latest evicts the
oldest when a stall fills it. That is the wrong end: the packet it
throws away is the one the worker is about to play, so every pop after
the queue fills leaves an RTP hole, and OpusDecoder::conceal_before
fills that hole with packet loss concealment, up to 100 ms of it.
Writing concealment takes exactly as long as the audio it stands in
for, so the worker falls further behind with every packet it takes.
The measured r0.47 session is that fixed point: 186 of the 200 packets
a second were dropped and the worker consumed 14, and 14 times 71 ms of
concealment is one second of audio a second. The sink is never idle,
the queue never drains, and 95 per cent of what the board played was
concealment noise.

A full queue now drops the packet that just arrived instead, so what
the worker pops stays contiguous and the queue drains. The drop is
reported as PushOutcome::DroppedNewest, which the platform counts under
the same linux-audio drop as before.

A self-inflicted hole is not loss either: the worker marks the decoder
when the queue reports a drop, and the next hole that appears is played
through instead of concealed. Holes longer than four packets are played
through as well, which covers any other way the playout can fall behind.
Holes of up to four packets are still concealed, which is what network
loss needs.

Greppable markers: opennow-audio-gap-skip
